# Node.js Standards

- Use modern Node.js and ES modules (`import`/`export`), not CommonJS.
- Prefer `async/await` over raw Promise chains or callbacks.
- Prefer small, focused modules and functions with descriptive names.
- Avoid deeply nested control flow — extract functions/early-return instead.
- Avoid giant files; split by responsibility (transport, service, provider).
- `index.js` is a startup/composition entry point only. It wires transports,
  services, and providers together — it must not contain application or
  Cookie Man logic.
- Use the official SDK for an external service (e.g. OpenAI, Twilio) rather
  than hand-rolling HTTP calls against their API.

## Comments

Comment intent, not mechanics.

- Explain *why* an architectural decision exists, surprising behavior,
  external-service constraints, security considerations, or unusual
  business rules.
- Do not add comments that just translate obvious code into English.

```javascript
// Good: explains why, not what
// Keep the AI provider injectable so tests never spend OpenAI tokens.

// Bad: narrates the obvious
// Set message equal to req.body.message.
const message = req.body.message;
```

## Error Handling

- Validate input at system boundaries (transport handlers).
- Return appropriate, useful HTTP status codes.
- Never leak raw upstream exceptions, stack traces, API keys, tokens, or
  config values to callers.
- Log enough context to diagnose failures, but never log secrets or
  unnecessary personal data.
- Handle failures from external APIs (OpenAI, Twilio) deliberately — don't
  let them bubble up unhandled.

## Dependencies

- Don't add a package if Node's platform APIs or an existing dependency
  already reasonably solve the problem.
- Before adding a dependency, ask whether it's actually necessary.
- Use maintained, widely-adopted packages for important infrastructure.
- Don't silently introduce major libraries or frameworks — call it out.
